VMware — Installation of Ubuntu Virtual Machine
Aim:
To install and configure Ubuntu operating system using VMware Workstation.
Procedure:
Step 1: Download VMware
1. Open a browser.
2. Go to VMware website.
3. Download VMware Workstation for Windows.
Step 2: Install VMware
1. Run the downloaded installer.
2. Follow the installation instructions.
3. Complete the installation.
Step 3: Download Ubuntu ISO File
1. Open browser.
2. Visit https://ubuntu.com
3. Download Ubuntu ISO file.
Step 4: Create a Virtual Machine
1. Open VMware.
2. Click Create a New Virtual Machine.
3. Select Typical (Recommended).
4. Click Next.
Step 5: Choose ISO File

Select the ISO image file.
1. Select Installer disc image file (ISO).
2. Click Browse.
3. Select your Ubuntu ISO file.
4. Click Next.
Step 6: Enter VM Name

Enter:
- Virtual machine name: Ubuntu VM
- Choose a location where you want to save the virtual machine.
- Example: D:\Virtual Machines\Ubuntu VM
- Click Next.
Step 7: Set Hard Disk Size

Enter:
- Size: 30 GB
- Store virtual disk as a single file.
- Click Next.
Step 8: Configure Hardware

Configure Hardware:
- CPU: 2 cores
- Memory: 4 GB
- Processor: 2 cores
Click Close.

Step 9: Start the Virtual Machine
1. Select your VM: Ubuntu-VM.
2. Click Power on this virtual machine.
3. Ubuntu will start.
Step 10: Restart the VM

After installation:
1. Click Restart Now.
2. Ubuntu will restart.
3. Login using the username and password you created.
Now your virtual machine is ready.

Step 11: Test a Virtual Machine
1. Open Terminal inside Ubuntu.
2. Run:
pwd


You should get something similar to:
/home/student


Check the operating system:
lsb_release -a


Check the IP Address:
ip addr


Test Internet:
ping google.com


If you receive responses such as:
64 bytes from google.com


The network is working.

Stop the ping using:
Ctrl + C


Step 12: Install VMware Tools

In Ubuntu Terminal, execute:
sudo apt update


Then:
sudo apt install open-vm-tools open-vm-tools-desktop -y


Restart:
sudo reboot
